      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2013 Nordic Pharmacological Society. Published by Blackwell Publishing Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": "Percutaneous transluminal angioplasty (PTA) with stenting is widely used in the treatment of vascular disorders, but restenosis remains a significant problem. Drug-eluting stents (DES) have been developed as an attempt to reduce the intimal response leading to restenosis. Drugs used in DES include mainly immunosuppressive and anti-proliferative compounds. Glucocorticoids are also an interesting possibility for those purposes because they have anti-proliferative effects in vascular smooth muscle cells and down-regulate the production of cytokines and growth factors driving inflammation and fibrosis. In this MiniReview, feasibility and safety of drug-eluting metal and biodegradable vascular stents are discussed with special emphasis on dexamethasone-eluting stents."
      },
